WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.820 --> 00:00:04.630
What do an icy despot and a crustacean-themed
conqueror have in common?

00:00:04.630 --> 00:00:08.260
"Game of Thrones" and "House of the Dragon"
viewers may notice some shared traits.

00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:11.330
Spoilers for "House of the Dragon" ahead.

00:00:11.330 --> 00:00:15.200
The ending of the second episode of "House
of the Dragon" introduces viewers to Craghas

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:18.960
Drahar, also known as "Crabfeeder," whose
nickname tells you everything you need to

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:22.150
know about his preferred method of execution
and body disposal.

00:00:22.150 --> 00:00:27.480
A high-ranking member of the Triarchy, Crabfeeder
has conquered the strategically important Stepstones islands.

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:32.090
As a result, the era's finest seafarer and
reigning Master of Ships, Lord Corlys Velaryon,

00:00:32.090 --> 00:00:33.090
isn't very happy.

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:38.789
"I will not have Driftmark beggared while
our king idles himself with feasts and balls

00:00:38.789 --> 00:00:39.789
and tourneys."

00:00:39.789 --> 00:00:44.280
The "Sea Snake" forms an alliance with disgraced
Daemon Targaryen, and in Episode 3, they face

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:46.540
off with Crabfeeder's forces on the Stepstones.

00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:51.411
In a desperate bid to get the win before reinforcements
come in, Daemon hatches a daring plan to use

00:00:51.411 --> 00:00:55.410
himself as bait and pretend to surrender to
draw Crabfeeder out from the caves he uses

00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:56.890
to avoid dragonfire.

00:00:56.890 --> 00:01:01.100
Against all odds, the desperate maneuver works
— and, after a thrilling battle sequence,

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:03.730
Daemon chases Crabfeeder down and kills him.

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:08.341
With his ravaged features and golden face
mask, Crabfeeder is a very memorable-looking guy.

00:01:08.341 --> 00:01:12.380
There's a time skip of three years in the
third episode of "House of the Dragon," and

00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:14.810
Crabfeeder has been holding the Stepstones
that whole time.

00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:18.040
Even so, he seems to go down easily when the
time comes.

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:21.009
Crabfeeder's quick end has some comparing
him to the "Game of Thrones" villain the Night

00:01:21.009 --> 00:01:22.820
King, and not in a very good way.

00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:26.470
They're both visually interesting and super
threatening villains who mostly just stand

00:01:26.470 --> 00:01:29.870
around looking creepy while everyone else
does the majority of the fighting.

00:01:29.870 --> 00:01:33.729
And then when it's time for them to die, they're
both dispatched fairly quickly.

00:01:33.729 --> 00:01:37.399
Apart from the fact that Crabfeeder isn't
a supernatural threat, there's of course one

00:01:37.399 --> 00:01:38.399
significant difference.

00:01:38.399 --> 00:01:42.540
By the time Arya Stark stabs the Night King
to death in "Game of Thrones," the villain

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:44.450
has been a looming threat since Season 4.

00:01:44.450 --> 00:01:48.869
As such, while the leader of the White Walkers
does go down over the course of a single episode,

00:01:48.869 --> 00:01:50.732
at least he's been around for a while.

00:01:50.732 --> 00:01:52.300
"What does he want?"

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:53.643
"An endless night.

00:01:55.385 --> 00:01:57.159
He wants to erase this world."

00:01:57.602 --> 00:02:01.090
Crabfeeder, on the other hand, only gets that
ominous intro at the end of Episode 2.

00:02:01.090 --> 00:02:04.790
The show then fast-forwards through all of
his cool moments, of which there must have

00:02:04.790 --> 00:02:08.360
been many, considering that he seems to have
been successfully holding the Stepstones for

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:09.880
several years.

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:11.900
We don't even see his gruesome death scene.

00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:14.620
Daemon bisects Crabfeeder offscreen.

00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:17.890
One would assume "House of the Dragon" is
trying to avoid making the mistakes many fans

00:02:17.890 --> 00:02:20.360
think "Game of Thrones" made in its final
season.

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:21.370
With author George R.R.

00:02:21.370 --> 00:02:25.210
Martin's increased involvement and the benefit
of hindsight, it's easy to imagine that the

00:02:25.210 --> 00:02:28.930
prequel will look to steer clear of the events
that led to rampant criticism of the last

00:02:28.930 --> 00:02:30.970
few episodes of "Game of Thrones."

00:02:30.970 --> 00:02:35.620
The Night King's swift and, for some, anticlimactic,
downfall after seasons of buildup seems like

00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:39.500
the kind of thing "House of the Dragon" wouldn't
be willing to touch with a ten-foot pole.

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:43.510
By introducing Crabfeeder in a "Big Bad" fashion
and doing away with the character after one

00:02:43.510 --> 00:02:47.650
episode of hectic battle, "House of the Dragon"
may deliberately be introducing similar-seeming

00:02:47.650 --> 00:02:50.970
storylines, but tweaking them so they have
a different impact.

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:54.450
It's also entirely possible that "House of
the Dragon" is using the shorthand of a similar

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:57.730
storyline as a means to build up Daemon as
a shrewd operator.

00:02:57.730 --> 00:03:01.360
Still, it's pretty strange that the show's
writers seemingly chose to call back to the

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:03.440
original show's least-loved era.

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:06.883
Unless it's unintentional, which could indicate
a whole different set of issues.

